WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.380
- Hello, everybody, I'm Kim Goff-Crews,

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:13.290
Secretary and Vice President
for University Life

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:14.930
and the Senior Officer in charge

00:00:14.930 --> 00:00:16.730
of the belonging at Yale Initiative.

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:22.350
This event is the first in a
series of events on anti racism

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:23.760
that is being done in conjunction

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:26.400
with the belonging at Yale Initiative.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.280
This is the university's major initiative

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:30.960
to create a climate of
belonging for all of us,

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:32.963
faculty, students, staff, and alumni.

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:36.700
The series is open to all staff, students,

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:38.240
faculty and alumni.

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:39.740
And on December 2nd,

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:44.240
we'll host our second
speaker, Dr. Ibram X. Kendi,

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:47.823
historian and author of
"How to Be an Antiracist."

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:52.410
Today, over 800 people
registered for this conversation,

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:53.930
and most of our guests are staff,

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:55.910
so a special welcome to you.

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:58.140
Many thanks to Deborah Stanley-McAulay,

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:01.870
who is the Associate Vice
President for employee engagement

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:04.580
and workplace culture,
and of course her team

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:06.630
for their generous support of this event.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:12.890
Our guest today is Dr. Dolly
Chugh, Social Psychologist,

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:15.770
NYU Stern School of Business
Associate Professor,

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:19.010
and the author of "The
Person You Mean to Be:

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:21.430
How Good People Fight Bias."

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:25.665
In her book, Dr. Chugh
provides practical applications

00:01:25.665 --> 00:01:28.670
to evidence based research

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:30.850
that can help us address our own biases

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:33.760
in our personal lives
and in our workplace.

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:36.550
She helps us understand
how we can be better

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:40.040
by practicing vulnerability,
understanding our privilege,

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:42.330
making mistakes, and learning from them

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:43.613
with a growth mindset.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:47.667
She reminds us that we have
things to learn, all of us.

00:01:47.667 --> 00:01:50.260
We're not always the
people who we mean to be

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:51.620
or even think we are.

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:54.170
But most of us are good
people trying to do better.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.400
Dr. Laurie Santos will
facilitate the conversation

00:01:58.400 --> 00:01:59.550
and incorporate questions

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:01.910
that have been submitted in advance.

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:03.050
Now she doesn't need an introduction,

00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:04.190
but I am gonna say a few words

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:06.770
for the few of you who don't know her.

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:10.040
Dr. Santos is a Yale
Professor of Psychology

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:12.370
and Head of Silliman College

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:15.980
She hosts the very popular
podcast, "The Happiness Lab"

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:19.270
and is the Director of the
Comparative Cognition Laboratory

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:22.100
and the Canine Cognition Center at Yale.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:24.880
Her course, psychology and the good life

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:27.993
is the most popular course
ever taught at Yale.

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:32.280
Is my pleasure to turn the
program over to Dr. Santos

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:33.900
to introduce Dr. Chugh,

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:35.410
and begin What I know is gonna be

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:37.573
a really informative conversation.

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:40.550
- Thank you so much, Kim,
for that introduction.

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:43.110
And thanks so much for
having me at this first panel

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:44.797
on belonging at Yale.

00:02:44.797 --> 00:02:46.255
I'm super thrilled to be part of it.

00:02:46.255 --> 00:02:48.490
Kim already gave you a quick introduction

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:50.050
to Dolly Chugh's work,

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:52.250
but since I love her and her work so much,

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:54.470
I couldn't help but kind of
tell you a little bit more

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:56.240
about what she's been up to.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:59.030
As mentioned, Dolly is
an Associate Professor

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:02.520
at NYU Stern School of Business.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:04.380
But her work really
focuses on this problem

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:05.960
of what it means to be a good person

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:07.520
and the psychological mechanisms

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:09.560
that make up being a good person.

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:11.120
Her book, "The Person You Mean to Be:

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:12.430
How Good People Fight Bias"

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:14.650
is one of my favorite books out there

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:16.630
and you should definitely check it out.

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:18.480
You should also check out her newsletter,

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:20.370
which is called Dear Good People,

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:23.700
you can sign up
@dollychugh.com/newsletter.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:25.360
And it is fantastic because

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:27.630
while her book is a whole host of insights

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:29.990
and her book length
worth of wonderful tips

00:03:29.990 --> 00:03:32.280
for how you can kind of
fight by us all the time,

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.240
her newsletter will give
you one actionable tip

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:35.820
every single time you receive it,

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:37.580
and it's a fantastic
way to kind of keep up

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:39.540
with your anti racism efforts.

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:42.620
But it's also worth noting just
how important Dolly has been

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:43.560
in this fight so far,

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:45.760
and just how much of an impact she's had.

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:46.680
As with most people,

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:48.770
Dolly has had an amazing
number of accolades

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:50.290
way too many to list here.

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:51.860
But it's worth noting that she is listed

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:54.300
as one of the top 100
most influential people

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:56.970
in Business Ethics by Ethisphere Magazine.

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:58.420
I'll point out that this is a list

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:00.490
that also includes
people like Pope Francis,

00:04:00.490 --> 00:04:02.080
Angelina Jolie and Bill Gates,

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:05.190
you get a sense of her
kind of influence here.

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:06.820
She's also an amazing teacher.

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:08.410
She's been one of the most highly rated

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:10.940
Business School professors
at NYU for a long time.

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:11.790
And just this year,

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:15.310
she received the 2020 NYU
Distinguished Teaching Award.

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:18.370
She's just an amazing
teacher and an amazing mentor

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:20.910
and an amazing person
in terms of her impact.

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:22.510
And today, we're really
gonna kind of dive deep

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:23.343
into her work.

00:04:23.343 --> 00:04:24.600
We're gonna talk not just about

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:26.460
the kinds of things we need to do

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:28.400
to focus on becoming better people,

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:30.380
but we're gonna explore
how her work demonstrates

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:32.150
that all of us really need to step it up

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:33.810
if we wanna kind of create the change

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:35.300
that we wanna see in our world.

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:38.070
If we wanna have just societies
and just organizations

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:41.600
and just universities, then we
really need to do something.

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:42.750
And one of the things we need to do

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:44.139
is to understand the biases

00:04:44.139 --> 00:04:45.670
that we're all walking around with.

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:48.340
We need to hack those biases
and understand how they work

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:50.320
so that we can become better people.

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:53.160
And so with that, I'm super
excited to introduce Dolly.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:54.750
I'm such a huge fan and I'm thrilled

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:56.280
that Yale has brought her here today.

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:58.493
Please join me in
welcoming Dr. Dolly Chugh.

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:01.013
- Hi, Laurie.

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:03.775
- Thanks so much for coming again.

00:05:03.775 --> 00:05:05.400
- Oh, my God!

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:06.520
- So I wanted to start out

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:08.060
with maybe what's an obvious question.

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:10.580
So, you know, we have a
lot of people on this call,

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:14.080
but I hope I can speak for
all of them when I say that

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:17.030
everybody on this call, like
truly cares about equity,

00:05:17.030 --> 00:05:18.840
like everybody on this call, I hope,

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:21.930
believes that people from
all races, all ethnicities,

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:24.570
all body statuses, all that stuff,

00:05:24.570 --> 00:05:26.310
should be treated equally.

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:27.790
But what you argue in your book

00:05:27.790 --> 00:05:30.560
is that that belief in equity isn't enough

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:32.470
and is, in fact, part of the problem.

00:05:32.470 --> 00:05:35.695
So, what we rewired
believing isn't enough.

00:05:35.695 --> 00:05:37.170
- Oh, okay. Great.

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:39.740
Thank you for having
me, Laurie, colleagues,

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:41.303
it's wonderful to be here.

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:44.340
Well, I talked about this difference

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:47.080
between being a believer and a builder.

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:49.580
And I say this from a place, frankly,

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:53.000
seeing myself in exactly
what I'm about to describe.

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:57.270
So, as you just listed,
there's a whole range of ways

00:05:57.270 --> 00:06:00.630
in which a lot of us want to see equality

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:03.150
and even equity in the world around us.

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:07.050
And we believe that's what should happen.

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:09.710
We also are believers,
that that doesn't exist.

00:06:09.710 --> 00:06:11.330
So we don't have to be convinced,

00:06:11.330 --> 00:06:14.160
we may not be deeply
informed on every issue

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:17.380
and every identity, but we
know that things aren't quite

00:06:17.380 --> 00:06:18.350
where we want them to be.

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:20.253
We're believers in that sense.

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:25.160
The challenge, however,
is that the status quo

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:28.480
doesn't change based on
our beliefs, necessarily,

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:33.260
particularly if were
really kind of invested

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:35.420
in being the kind of
people who believe that

00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:39.330
and I think I am, I really
wanna be the kind of person

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:43.710
who treats people well,
that creates opportunities

00:06:43.710 --> 00:06:45.860
for people who haven't had opportunities,

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:47.593
I wanna be that kind of believer.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:53.960
However, in order to do it, I
have to build certain things,

00:06:53.960 --> 00:06:57.630
I have to build knowledge,
I have to build skills,

00:06:57.630 --> 00:07:01.950
I have to build systems,
I have to build courage,

00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:03.420
I have to build habits,

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:08.160
these are all active modes of operating,

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:11.650
that won't happen just
because I believe something.

00:07:11.650 --> 00:07:16.497
So just because I believe every
student in my class at NYU,

00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:20.360
should be treated equally,
should have an equal opportunity

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:22.120
to succeed in my class,

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:25.120
that doesn't mean that
I have built a system

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:26.430
in which that can happen.

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:28.130
And with that, I built a classroom

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:31.680
in which everyone feels equally welcome.

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:33.700
That I built a grading protocol

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:37.000
in which everyone has
the possibility to excel,

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:38.850
that I've built a culture

00:07:38.850 --> 00:07:41.040
in which people feel
they can come talk to me

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:42.250
if they have a question.

00:07:42.250 --> 00:07:44.670
Those are all actions I have to take.

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:46.690
So the distinction between
believing and building

00:07:46.690 --> 00:07:48.200
are really, I shouldn't
call it distinction

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:50.880
that continuum between moving
through being a believer

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:54.650
to a builder, for me is
about taking actions.

00:07:54.650 --> 00:07:58.250
And the challenge with
when we don't do that,

00:07:58.250 --> 00:08:01.350
when we sit in that
believer end of the pool,

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:03.050
is that we get comfortable.

00:08:03.050 --> 00:08:05.520
We care so much about this issue,

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:07.620
that we develop an identity

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:09.970
as being someone supportive of this issue,

00:08:09.970 --> 00:08:11.630
but not doing anything about it.

00:08:11.630 --> 00:08:14.020
And if someone says, "Hey, how about...

00:08:14.020 --> 00:08:18.090
I noticed the ways in which
maybe you're contributing

00:08:18.090 --> 00:08:18.940
to the challenge..."

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:22.727
Like if my teaching assistant says to me,

00:08:22.727 --> 00:08:25.520
"I noticed that your calling patterns

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:28.600
reflect you calling on
men more than women."

00:08:28.600 --> 00:08:31.758
Which happens. That's true.

00:08:31.758 --> 00:08:34.397
Then I'm gonna get
defensive, because I'm like,

00:08:34.397 --> 00:08:36.997
"Well, no, I believe in
equity in my classroom."

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:41.860
Rather than building saying,
"Ah, okay, now I have data.

00:08:41.860 --> 00:08:43.300
What can I do with this data?

00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:45.580
What can I do to change
my calling patterns?

00:08:45.580 --> 00:08:47.290
What can I do to make it possible

00:08:47.290 --> 00:08:48.930
for more people to have their hands up?"

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:50.590
That's what the builder responses

00:08:50.590 --> 00:08:52.740
as opposed to the believer response.

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:55.860
- And I think this is so
important, because again, you know,

00:08:55.860 --> 00:08:57.510
I'm sure everybody who's coming to this

00:08:57.510 --> 00:09:00.320
is coming from this place of
like caring about this stuff.

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:01.153
- Yes.

00:09:01.153 --> 00:09:03.160
- I think that's super
important that the caring alone,

00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:05.190
it's not just like, not helpful,

00:09:05.190 --> 00:09:06.940
it could actually be
deeply counterproductive

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:08.210
to some interesting extent.

00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:10.610
- And in fact, it feels like abandonment,

00:09:10.610 --> 00:09:13.680
I think, when we're part of the groups

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:15.809
that are dealing with this,

00:09:15.809 --> 00:09:19.940
if you're a black student at Yale,

00:09:19.940 --> 00:09:23.410
and your white classmates
believe in all this

00:09:23.410 --> 00:09:27.070
but aren't taking action on
it, it feels like abandonment.

00:09:27.070 --> 00:09:28.610
It doesn't feel like supportive.

00:09:28.610 --> 00:09:30.850
Oh, I am not, of course
speaking for everyone,

00:09:30.850 --> 00:09:34.800
but in general that's the perception

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:37.710
that both science and
anecdotes would suggest.

00:09:37.710 --> 00:09:39.780
So, that's the danger of being a believer

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:42.970
is that it feels shallow,

00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:44.880
we're in the shallow end of the pool.

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:46.500
- And so you've talked about
a couple different ways

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:49.020
that we can kind of dive into
the deeper end of the pool.

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:52.550
That there's kind of
different ways to be a builder

00:09:52.550 --> 00:09:54.960
with a kind of like
really hardcore building

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:56.900
and it kind of softer type of building.

00:09:56.900 --> 00:09:59.190
So talk about, in your book,
you make this distinction

00:09:59.190 --> 00:10:00.310
between light and heat.

00:10:00.310 --> 00:10:01.930
Which I think is a really great analogy.

00:10:01.930 --> 00:10:04.360
So walk me through kind of
the light version of building

00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:06.240
versus the heat version of building.

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:07.780
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:10:07.780 --> 00:10:09.370
And I'm gonna preface this by saying

00:10:09.370 --> 00:10:11.720
it's the most important
thing about light and heat

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:14.040
is to know that we need both.

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:15.680
Let me define what each of them mean.

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:17.900
I'm using a metaphor, I
wasn't able to track down

00:10:17.900 --> 00:10:20.300
who originally came up with
this light/heat metaphor

00:10:20.300 --> 00:10:22.056
is widely used.

00:10:22.056 --> 00:10:25.870
The idea of, when you take
a light based approach

00:10:25.870 --> 00:10:29.490
to let's say, making
change in your community,

00:10:29.490 --> 00:10:33.290
or in your institution, you
meet people where they are,

00:10:33.290 --> 00:10:38.290
you center their comfort,
you educate, you teach.

00:10:39.610 --> 00:10:43.333
It's slower, and it's more incremental.

00:10:44.670 --> 00:10:46.880
A heat based approach to making change

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:49.340
is one in which by definition

00:10:49.340 --> 00:10:51.560
is not centering the comfort of others,

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:55.700
it is pushing, it is disruptive.

00:10:55.700 --> 00:10:58.880
And it's meant to push for change faster

00:10:58.880 --> 00:11:01.330
in a more dramatic ways.

00:11:01.330 --> 00:11:04.740
Most of us, my sense is kind of lean

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:07.150
in terms of our personal preferences

00:11:07.150 --> 00:11:09.470
towards more light or more heat.

00:11:09.470 --> 00:11:11.560
Some people are good at both
and comfortable with both.

00:11:11.560 --> 00:11:13.270
But like I would say,

00:11:13.270 --> 00:11:17.400
I tend to lean towards the
light based approaches.

00:11:17.400 --> 00:11:19.480
But this was a really,

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:22.100
I would say, probably the
most important learning

00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:25.487
I had from writing this
book was realizing that

00:11:25.487 --> 00:11:28.860
just because I am more
comfortable with light,

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:30.730
doesn't mean that I don't,

00:11:30.730 --> 00:11:32.880
I shouldn't see the value in heat.

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:34.860
What historians have showed us,

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:36.929
historians of social movements,

00:11:36.929 --> 00:11:39.680
is that, when a movement sort
of leans heavily on light,

00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:41.760
or leans heavily on heat,

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:44.850
versus movements that balance both,

00:11:44.850 --> 00:11:48.143
the movements that balance
both make more progress.

00:11:49.070 --> 00:11:51.703
So, the message here,
and this is why I feel

00:11:51.703 --> 00:11:53.580
it's so important for me to learn this

00:11:53.580 --> 00:11:57.220
is just because I am a light based person

00:11:57.220 --> 00:12:00.430
doesn't mean that I
shouldn't be cheering on

00:12:00.430 --> 00:12:02.290
the heat based folks.

00:12:02.290 --> 00:12:06.210
And just because I'm a heat based person,

00:12:06.210 --> 00:12:09.020
doesn't mean I shouldn't be
cheering on the light base.

00:12:09.020 --> 00:12:13.230
We often feel the tactics
being used by the others

00:12:13.230 --> 00:12:15.980
aren't what we would do
and aren't as effective,

00:12:15.980 --> 00:12:19.520
but one of the differences
that light and heat has

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:20.460
and why we need both

00:12:20.460 --> 00:12:23.730
is that light may be more
likely to change minds,

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:26.500
but it's probably not
gonna change systems.

00:12:26.500 --> 00:12:29.420
Heat is probably more
likely to change systems

00:12:29.420 --> 00:12:31.330
and less likely to change minds.

00:12:31.330 --> 00:12:32.520
Well, we need both.

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:37.390
We need both our minds
and our systems to move

00:12:37.390 --> 00:12:39.210
in order for real change to happen.

00:12:39.210 --> 00:12:43.030
And so, I think in this
work of being a builder,

00:12:43.030 --> 00:12:46.790
sometimes we preference, you
know, because I'm right handed,

00:12:46.790 --> 00:12:48.120
I want everyone to be right handed

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:50.110
or because I'm left, I
want everybody left handed.

00:12:50.110 --> 00:12:52.980
But know, what we want is like bass people

00:12:52.980 --> 00:12:54.290
to cheer on the heat bass people

00:12:54.290 --> 00:12:56.370
and heat bass people to cheer
on the light bass people.

00:12:56.370 --> 00:12:58.190
And frankly, it's all good.

00:12:58.190 --> 00:12:59.430
Wherever you are in that space,

00:12:59.430 --> 00:13:01.490
as long as you're not in the inaction.

00:13:01.490 --> 00:13:03.150
You know, that's back to being a believer,

00:13:03.150 --> 00:13:05.770
I don't know, keep the
metaphor going like dark,

00:13:05.770 --> 00:13:07.993
notice if you will, or
silence or something.

00:13:08.830 --> 00:13:11.610
But the light and heat are both important.

00:13:11.610 --> 00:13:15.097
It's particularly important
that we cheer on the heat.

00:13:15.097 --> 00:13:16.270
I don't have data on this,

00:13:16.270 --> 00:13:20.340
but I bet there's more light
base people in your community,

00:13:20.340 --> 00:13:21.900
than there are heat based.

00:13:21.900 --> 00:13:23.810
And boy, you should be grateful

00:13:23.810 --> 00:13:25.940
that you have those heat base, folks.

00:13:25.940 --> 00:13:26.773
- That's perfect.

00:13:26.773 --> 00:13:29.027
And so, I mean, so both of us
need to kind of as you said,

00:13:29.027 --> 00:13:29.860
"Get past the darkness."

00:13:29.860 --> 00:13:31.440
Like we need the light heat,

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:33.540
but people can be sort of
sitting around in the dark.

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:34.560
And so-
- Right.

00:13:34.560 --> 00:13:37.630
- One of the first skill
sets you present in the book

00:13:37.630 --> 00:13:39.190
for getting people out of the darkness,

00:13:39.190 --> 00:13:41.200
and again, the darkness is
believing in equity, right?

00:13:41.200 --> 00:13:43.850
It's like things just
like that (indistinct)

00:13:43.850 --> 00:13:46.990
The first step is really kind
of learning something, right?

00:13:46.990 --> 00:13:49.550
It's actually recognizing
the sorts of privileges

00:13:49.550 --> 00:13:50.383
that you have.

00:13:50.383 --> 00:13:52.160
And so, just give me a tricky concept

00:13:52.160 --> 00:13:54.160
for people who haven't talked
a lot about this before.

00:13:54.160 --> 00:13:57.290
So walk me through this idea
of privilege, what that means.

00:13:57.290 --> 00:14:00.750
- Yeah, I struggle with this too.

00:14:00.750 --> 00:14:04.510
It's tied a lot to another
tricky word, which is systemic.

00:14:04.510 --> 00:14:06.460
I think these words go hand in hand

00:14:06.460 --> 00:14:09.270
and are hard to understand.

00:14:09.270 --> 00:14:11.180
The metaphor that helped me with that

00:14:11.180 --> 00:14:12.740
comes from Debbie Irving.

00:14:12.740 --> 00:14:15.520
She uses a metaphor of
headwinds and tailwind

00:14:15.520 --> 00:14:17.093
that I find really useful.

00:14:18.170 --> 00:14:19.700
So, I'm looking over there

00:14:19.700 --> 00:14:21.120
to make sure my kids aren't eavesdropping,

00:14:21.120 --> 00:14:22.110
'cause they'll always laugh

00:14:22.110 --> 00:14:24.137
with the little example
I'm about to give you.

00:14:24.137 --> 00:14:28.050
So the example is, let's
say I was to go running...

00:14:28.050 --> 00:14:29.410
That's when they start laughing.

00:14:29.410 --> 00:14:31.410
Like, "Oh, let's say..."

00:14:31.410 --> 00:14:34.950
So let's say I go running and
I say, "Okay, I'm gonna run

00:14:34.950 --> 00:14:38.020
to the high school on the
corner, have a good jog,

00:14:38.020 --> 00:14:39.240
and then I'm gonna u-turn

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:41.540
and come right back the way I came."

00:14:41.540 --> 00:14:44.070
And while I'm making my way over there,

00:14:44.070 --> 00:14:45.610
I'm feeling pretty good.

00:14:45.610 --> 00:14:46.945
I'm going, you know,

00:14:46.945 --> 00:14:49.010
I think there's no carb
things working out.

00:14:49.010 --> 00:14:52.690
I'm feeling pretty strong,
you know, make a good time.

00:14:52.690 --> 00:14:54.710
And then I turn around for the U turn

00:14:54.710 --> 00:14:58.850
and suddenly things feel
harder and more effortful.

00:14:58.850 --> 00:15:03.550
And if someone's was just like
inside their home or office

00:15:03.550 --> 00:15:06.000
and watching me through a window,

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:08.000
they might, if they
just see me coming back,

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:10.720
be like, "Doesn't even look
like she's trying that hard

00:15:10.720 --> 00:15:12.760
or she doesn't look that motivated

00:15:12.760 --> 00:15:14.240
or I don't know she
comes from a community,

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:15.650
doesn't really value running.

00:15:15.650 --> 00:15:18.690
I see she's just not taking it seriously."

00:15:18.690 --> 00:15:23.420
They wouldn't see what I'm
feeling which is a headwind

00:15:23.420 --> 00:15:25.910
in my face on the way back.

00:15:25.910 --> 00:15:27.560
And on my way there,

00:15:27.560 --> 00:15:29.940
that tailwind which was
propelling me forward,

00:15:29.940 --> 00:15:32.930
I didn't even feel 'cause you
don't really feel tailwinds,

00:15:32.930 --> 00:15:35.520
you benefit from them,
but you don't feel them.

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:38.600
And so, the headwinds are more easily felt

00:15:38.600 --> 00:15:39.773
than the tailwinds.

00:15:40.628 --> 00:15:43.980
And all of it is sort
of invisible in general

00:15:43.980 --> 00:15:46.090
and hard to see.

00:15:46.090 --> 00:15:48.250
And that's, I think, a
good way to think about

00:15:48.250 --> 00:15:52.350
what privilege is, is
that at any given moment,

00:15:52.350 --> 00:15:54.920
any one of us has some form of privilege

00:15:54.920 --> 00:15:56.780
on some form of our their identities.

00:15:56.780 --> 00:15:59.680
Like, "I'm straight.

00:15:59.680 --> 00:16:02.230
And while I don't spend a lot of time

00:16:02.230 --> 00:16:03.280
thinking about my straightness,

00:16:03.280 --> 00:16:04.890
because frankly I don't have to.

00:16:04.890 --> 00:16:07.310
The world is kind of, police community

00:16:07.310 --> 00:16:08.674
in the society I live in

00:16:08.674 --> 00:16:10.730
makes it pretty easy to be straight.

00:16:10.730 --> 00:16:13.390
It doesn't create obstacles for me,

00:16:13.390 --> 00:16:15.240
I have lots of legal rights.

00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:17.147
I have lots of physical safety."

00:16:18.780 --> 00:16:21.010
That privilege that I have,

00:16:21.010 --> 00:16:23.940
that tailwind is one I don't
even really think about

00:16:24.830 --> 00:16:28.640
versus the headwind that
if someone isn't straight,

00:16:28.640 --> 00:16:31.360
they're constantly navigating.

00:16:31.360 --> 00:16:33.080
And so when we talk about privilege,

00:16:33.080 --> 00:16:34.820
what we're really talking about

00:16:34.820 --> 00:16:37.950
are where do we have tailwinds.

00:16:37.950 --> 00:16:40.530
And each of us have multiple identities

00:16:40.530 --> 00:16:44.180
and therefore multiple
headwinds and tailwinds.

00:16:44.180 --> 00:16:47.963
Right now, I think in 2020,
we've had an opportunity.

00:16:48.930 --> 00:16:53.053
There's been headwinds, if
you are a black American,

00:16:54.420 --> 00:16:56.970
there are headwinds that
have been very visible

00:16:56.970 --> 00:16:59.870
and felt by people in the black community

00:16:59.870 --> 00:17:03.800
that haven't been felt
and seen and experienced

00:17:03.800 --> 00:17:05.590
by many people outside
the black community.

00:17:05.590 --> 00:17:08.440
And that became visible in a new way

00:17:08.440 --> 00:17:11.330
in this year for many people,
some people already know.

00:17:11.330 --> 00:17:16.330
So that privilege is sort
of one in which we're now

00:17:16.520 --> 00:17:18.950
tuning into in a way that maybe

00:17:18.950 --> 00:17:20.950
at a widespread countrywide basis,

00:17:20.950 --> 00:17:22.950
we haven't done in the past.

00:17:22.950 --> 00:17:23.900
- But this is kind of the key

00:17:23.900 --> 00:17:25.320
of these headwinds and tailwinds

00:17:25.320 --> 00:17:27.931
is that the both of them are invisible.

00:17:27.931 --> 00:17:30.390
I guess the headwinds are very visible,

00:17:30.390 --> 00:17:31.966
if you're being slowed down by them

00:17:31.966 --> 00:17:34.070
they're incredibly visible to you.

00:17:34.070 --> 00:17:36.350
But if you're not from
a marginalized identity,

00:17:36.350 --> 00:17:38.800
or one of your identities
is not marginalized,

00:17:38.800 --> 00:17:40.053
you just don't see it.

00:17:41.345 --> 00:17:42.730
What I love about your
book is that you point out

00:17:42.730 --> 00:17:44.210
that this is true for you, too, right?

00:17:44.210 --> 00:17:45.880
Like you do out on it.

00:17:45.880 --> 00:17:48.130
And it's still hard to see these things.

00:17:48.130 --> 00:17:48.963
- Absolutely.

00:17:48.963 --> 00:17:52.060
Like I have so many
tailwinds and, you know,

00:17:52.060 --> 00:17:54.980
whether it's me not realizing, you know,

00:17:54.980 --> 00:17:58.160
I'm the child of Indian immigrants.

00:17:58.160 --> 00:17:59.410
So I was born in India,

00:17:59.410 --> 00:18:01.970
but a baby when my parents came here,

00:18:01.970 --> 00:18:05.750
and, you know, I don't know what...

00:18:05.750 --> 00:18:07.450
Like, I'm not sure how people identify me,

00:18:07.450 --> 00:18:10.083
I think I pass as a lot of things.

00:18:11.240 --> 00:18:12.540
That privileges me.

00:18:12.540 --> 00:18:15.370
I get considered a person
of color in some circles

00:18:15.370 --> 00:18:17.090
in a way that privileges me

00:18:17.090 --> 00:18:18.570
when I'm with other people of color.

00:18:18.570 --> 00:18:21.580
And I think some people
see me as white maybe,

00:18:21.580 --> 00:18:25.180
and I sort of pass in those circles.

00:18:25.180 --> 00:18:26.560
There's lots of ways, you know,

00:18:26.560 --> 00:18:28.610
the straightness is another example.

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:32.190
I think, you know, my education
level, my native language,

00:18:32.190 --> 00:18:34.100
I'm cisgender.

00:18:34.100 --> 00:18:36.980
None of these identities are
better than other identities.

00:18:36.980 --> 00:18:39.930
I'm not in any way saying I'm lucky

00:18:39.930 --> 00:18:41.140
to have those identities,

00:18:41.140 --> 00:18:45.300
but I do incur benefits as a
result of those identities,

00:18:45.300 --> 00:18:47.303
my education, my income level,

00:18:49.369 --> 00:18:53.000
I'm constantly surprised at
the things I'm oblivious to,

00:18:53.000 --> 00:18:56.560
you know, that the fact, you know,

00:18:56.560 --> 00:19:01.560
most recently, the set of
awareness that I've gained

00:19:01.930 --> 00:19:05.533
is around my physical
abilities and disabilities.

00:19:06.450 --> 00:19:09.250
And realizing there's
lots of ways in which

00:19:09.250 --> 00:19:13.100
I have just not even noticed
how the world is not set up

00:19:13.100 --> 00:19:17.660
for students in my community
in my classes who are disabled.

00:19:17.660 --> 00:19:19.540
And that's despite supposedly,

00:19:19.540 --> 00:19:21.410
like legal accommodations that we make,

00:19:21.410 --> 00:19:23.350
that really, I can tell
you in my classroom

00:19:23.350 --> 00:19:24.490
have not been sufficient.

00:19:24.490 --> 00:19:27.800
And so, I've just recently
realized there's all these ways

00:19:27.800 --> 00:19:30.047
in which I've made learning
harder for my students

00:19:30.047 --> 00:19:32.240
that I could have been addressing,

00:19:32.240 --> 00:19:33.983
like using captions on Zoom.

00:19:35.010 --> 00:19:37.730
Like when we were teaching in person

00:19:37.730 --> 00:19:39.600
using captions on videos.

00:19:39.600 --> 00:19:43.033
I mean, those are just too many ways.

00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:47.510
So these privileges that
are like that's a headwind

00:19:47.510 --> 00:19:50.620
I was just not seeing at all.

00:19:50.620 --> 00:19:52.340
Yeah, and so when you
bring up those examples,

00:19:52.340 --> 00:19:55.330
I think, you know, everybody
who has those cases

00:19:55.330 --> 00:19:57.957
where they kind of
notice, like spots where,

00:19:57.957 --> 00:20:00.180
"Wow, I was just having it so much easier

00:20:00.180 --> 00:20:02.590
than I even realized that I'm
making inadvertently harder

00:20:02.590 --> 00:20:03.423
for the people around me."

00:20:03.423 --> 00:20:04.440
And I had some hand in it, right?

00:20:04.440 --> 00:20:05.739
It's not just the system like,

00:20:05.739 --> 00:20:09.750
the videos I posted for my
class had that same feature.

00:20:09.750 --> 00:20:12.140
Sometimes, like one bad
thing about privilege

00:20:12.140 --> 00:20:13.190
is that it's invisible.

00:20:13.190 --> 00:20:14.480
But the second bad thing about privilege

00:20:14.480 --> 00:20:18.500
is that when you notice it, it
feels incredibly bad, right?

00:20:18.500 --> 00:20:20.670
Like, even in cases where
you didn't have a hand in it,

00:20:20.670 --> 00:20:22.040
like you feel kind of awful.

00:20:22.040 --> 00:20:24.900
Like, I didn't try to get these tail winds

00:20:24.900 --> 00:20:27.600
that are making me speed past everyone.

00:20:27.600 --> 00:20:31.200
And so, talk about that
feature of privilege, right?

00:20:31.200 --> 00:20:34.120
'Cause I think, you know, a
lot of us don't like feeling

00:20:34.120 --> 00:20:35.800
like we're getting these tail winds that

00:20:35.800 --> 00:20:37.200
are kind of helping us out a lot.

00:20:37.200 --> 00:20:39.740
And what does that do to our psychology?

00:20:39.740 --> 00:20:42.720
- Yeah, I mean, it's
pretty mortifying, right.

00:20:42.720 --> 00:20:44.760
And there's research that shows

00:20:44.760 --> 00:20:49.600
that we actually, we like to get things

00:20:49.600 --> 00:20:52.090
but we sort of like to feel
like we do we deserve it

00:20:52.090 --> 00:20:52.980
and we earned it, right.

00:20:52.980 --> 00:20:56.920
Like, we don't like to feel
like we unfairly got something.

00:20:56.920 --> 00:20:59.480
And so, yeah, it feels awful.

00:20:59.480 --> 00:21:02.790
I think where we make a lot of headway,

00:21:02.790 --> 00:21:05.210
when we have those moments of insight

00:21:05.210 --> 00:21:08.320
is if we can sort of in
the mental accounting

00:21:08.320 --> 00:21:11.263
of that noticing, put
it in the growth bucket.

00:21:12.280 --> 00:21:13.610
This is a place of growth.

00:21:13.610 --> 00:21:17.340
And so, you know, one thing
I've been talking about lately

00:21:17.340 --> 00:21:21.680
is like, if you can't think
about a TV show, or a joke,

00:21:21.680 --> 00:21:26.680
or word that you watched or
used or laughed at, a year ago,

00:21:26.940 --> 00:21:30.740
or five years ago, that
you're now mortified by,

00:21:30.740 --> 00:21:33.720
I'm gonna argue that you're not growing,

00:21:33.720 --> 00:21:34.860
you're not learning.

00:21:34.860 --> 00:21:37.310
If you feel like everything
you've ever said,

00:21:37.310 --> 00:21:40.910
done or watched is sort of
aligned with who you are now

00:21:40.910 --> 00:21:42.490
and what you know now,

00:21:42.490 --> 00:21:46.070
that says to me, there's like
this very static, you know,

00:21:46.070 --> 00:21:51.020
brittle, almost good person
identity that isn't evolving.

00:21:51.020 --> 00:21:52.410
In most aspects of our life,

00:21:52.410 --> 00:21:54.503
we try to get better all the time.

00:21:56.490 --> 00:21:59.510
You try a recipe you haven't
tried before, it work,

00:21:59.510 --> 00:22:02.223
you try to master a
software system or whatever.

00:22:03.070 --> 00:22:05.500
In this one area of being
a good person, though,

00:22:05.500 --> 00:22:10.500
we view every mistake as
a complete condemnation,

00:22:11.430 --> 00:22:13.540
a complete sort of blanket of shame.

00:22:13.540 --> 00:22:17.240
You know, I sort of feel
myself kind of get warm

00:22:17.240 --> 00:22:18.973
and sweaty in those moments.

00:22:20.020 --> 00:22:21.920
But what's really helped me

00:22:21.920 --> 00:22:25.220
and the research by Carol
Dweck, psychologists

00:22:25.220 --> 00:22:26.990
and her co author supports this,

00:22:26.990 --> 00:22:29.760
is if we can view things as growth,

00:22:29.760 --> 00:22:33.560
not sort of in a fixed
mindset as she describes it,

00:22:33.560 --> 00:22:35.450
in which we have nowhere to go,

00:22:35.450 --> 00:22:38.630
but we actually view, "Oh, I just noticed,

00:22:38.630 --> 00:22:41.810
like those videos, that
was deeply problematic.

00:22:41.810 --> 00:22:45.040
That was something I should have known.

00:22:45.040 --> 00:22:46.270
I shouldn't have had...

00:22:46.270 --> 00:22:47.360
By the way, that was an email

00:22:47.360 --> 00:22:49.080
that I probably dismissed about it.

00:22:49.080 --> 00:22:51.477
Like I really had to be nudged."

00:22:53.021 --> 00:22:54.777
That's a moment for me to say,

00:22:54.777 --> 00:22:57.480
"Okay, I now know more
than I did a year ago,

00:22:57.480 --> 00:23:00.320
and five years ago, and I'm acting on it."

00:23:00.320 --> 00:23:02.270
So I think we miss an opportunity

00:23:02.270 --> 00:23:04.690
to actually feel great about
ourselves in that moment.

00:23:04.690 --> 00:23:06.910
Not feel great that we noticed and stopped

00:23:06.910 --> 00:23:09.386
but that we noticed and learned.

00:23:09.386 --> 00:23:10.320
(indistinct)

00:23:10.320 --> 00:23:12.510
- This idea that you talked
a lot about in your book

00:23:12.510 --> 00:23:14.880
about not wanting to be a good person,

00:23:14.880 --> 00:23:16.300
but being a good ish person,

00:23:16.300 --> 00:23:17.660
which I think people can misconstrue

00:23:17.660 --> 00:23:19.180
when you say, "Don't worry
about being a good person

00:23:19.180 --> 00:23:22.080
when it comes to justice."

00:23:22.080 --> 00:23:24.340
And explain to me what
you mean by good ish

00:23:24.340 --> 00:23:25.610
'cause I think it's so important.

00:23:25.610 --> 00:23:29.270
- Yeah, thank you. I I've
got to be more precise.

00:23:29.270 --> 00:23:31.450
I've learned on that than
I've been in the past.

00:23:31.450 --> 00:23:34.340
Okay, so the campaign Ahmad

00:23:34.340 --> 00:23:36.500
is to get us to stop
trying to be good people.

00:23:36.500 --> 00:23:40.330
But that's not because I want
us to lower our standards.

00:23:40.330 --> 00:23:42.587
I'm actually making the
opposite argument that

00:23:42.587 --> 00:23:45.377
in our good person identities
are a fixed mindset identity.

00:23:45.377 --> 00:23:47.170
They're deeply brittle.

00:23:47.170 --> 00:23:51.720
There's no stretch there
to grow and get better.

00:23:51.720 --> 00:23:54.020
In a growth mindset, which in this domain,

00:23:54.020 --> 00:23:56.230
I would say it's called being goodish.

00:23:56.230 --> 00:23:58.910
We're actually actively
looking for those mistakes.

00:23:58.910 --> 00:24:00.030
It's an Easter egg hunt.

00:24:00.030 --> 00:24:02.243
They're out there, go find the candy.

00:24:03.460 --> 00:24:05.390
Candy. That's the wrong.
That was a terrible metaphor.

00:24:05.390 --> 00:24:09.230
But, go find the learnings.

00:24:09.230 --> 00:24:12.860
Because when you do, that's
where the growth happens.

00:24:12.860 --> 00:24:15.570
And so this goodish, what
I'm defining as good ish,

00:24:15.570 --> 00:24:18.800
is being someone who's actively noticing

00:24:18.800 --> 00:24:21.210
and actually seeking out those learnings.

00:24:21.210 --> 00:24:24.010
So, you know, we know from the
studies around growth mindset

00:24:24.010 --> 00:24:27.270
that when you make a
mistake in a fixed mindset,

00:24:27.270 --> 00:24:29.860
we actually see brain
activity kind of shut down,

00:24:29.860 --> 00:24:31.610
you actually don't attend to the mistake,

00:24:31.610 --> 00:24:34.560
you literally withdraw mental attention.

00:24:34.560 --> 00:24:35.970
Versus if you're in a growth mindset,

00:24:35.970 --> 00:24:36.803
and you make that mistake,

00:24:36.803 --> 00:24:39.680
we see all sorts of activity in the brain

00:24:39.680 --> 00:24:41.910
in a way that suggests
like, you may be mortified,

00:24:41.910 --> 00:24:44.010
but boy, are you going
to soak up what you can

00:24:44.010 --> 00:24:45.150
from this moment?

00:24:45.150 --> 00:24:46.970
And that's soaking up what you can

00:24:46.970 --> 00:24:48.310
that's what I mean by goodish.

00:24:48.310 --> 00:24:50.720
I don't mean like, oh, too bad.

00:24:50.720 --> 00:24:53.340
Like, I guess I'm not, you know,
I mean, the exact opposite.

00:24:53.340 --> 00:24:57.500
Like I am gonna own this.
Thank you for telling me that.

00:24:57.500 --> 00:24:59.400
Oh, wow. I had no idea.

00:24:59.400 --> 00:25:00.890
I'm gonna go look that up,

00:25:00.890 --> 00:25:03.360
I appreciate you bringing
it to my attention.

00:25:03.360 --> 00:25:06.250
That's the kind of outcome.

00:25:06.250 --> 00:25:07.375
- And that's where this idea

00:25:07.375 --> 00:25:11.450
that kind of feeling like
we've messed up, feels yucky,

00:25:11.450 --> 00:25:14.440
is problematic because one
thing can cause us to do is,

00:25:14.440 --> 00:25:16.890
if we don't have mechanisms
to have a growth mindset

00:25:16.890 --> 00:25:18.750
and kind of get over that yucky feeling,

00:25:18.750 --> 00:25:20.650
it can cause us to do
a number of bad things.

00:25:20.650 --> 00:25:23.700
One of them is just to deny
that we've messed up at all.

00:25:23.700 --> 00:25:25.040
Right?
- Right.

00:25:25.040 --> 00:25:25.970
Absolutely. Yeah.

00:25:25.970 --> 00:25:29.570
And that's, of course,
you know, the most harmful

00:25:29.570 --> 00:25:32.440
because not only are we not learning,

00:25:32.440 --> 00:25:35.830
if the context in which that
happening is someone saying,

00:25:35.830 --> 00:25:39.760
if I have a student saying to me,

00:25:39.760 --> 00:25:41.920
I think that reading you assign,

00:25:41.920 --> 00:25:45.030
like, there was just
nothing but white voices

00:25:45.030 --> 00:25:47.180
represented in that reading.

00:25:47.180 --> 00:25:48.617
And I kind of like dismiss,

00:25:48.617 --> 00:25:51.362
"Well, you don't understand my field."

00:25:51.362 --> 00:25:53.500
Which, by the way, is exactly the dialogue

00:25:53.500 --> 00:25:55.290
that goes through my mind.

00:25:55.290 --> 00:25:57.403
How dare you and don't you know,

00:25:58.350 --> 00:25:59.970
but when I can sort of like poop,

00:25:59.970 --> 00:26:01.310
there it is, those are my red flags.

00:26:01.310 --> 00:26:02.210
Those are my triggers.

00:26:02.210 --> 00:26:06.020
That's my good person responding.

00:26:06.020 --> 00:26:10.450
When I can flip it, you
know, to not deny them,

00:26:10.450 --> 00:26:13.720
I'm actually also now creating a space

00:26:13.720 --> 00:26:18.290
where their lived experience is valued

00:26:19.320 --> 00:26:20.970
as opposed to doing double harm.

00:26:20.970 --> 00:26:22.490
First, I made the mistake.

00:26:22.490 --> 00:26:23.570
And then I told them,

00:26:23.570 --> 00:26:26.024
their lived experience wasn't legitimate.

00:26:26.024 --> 00:26:28.807
I said to them, "Well, it's
not raining where I am.

00:26:28.807 --> 00:26:31.000
So I guess it's not
raining where you are."

00:26:31.000 --> 00:26:33.350
That's what I do with denial.

00:26:33.350 --> 00:26:34.183
- And the key with that

00:26:34.183 --> 00:26:36.310
is that you have to have this vision,

00:26:36.310 --> 00:26:38.020
first of all, but you
don't have to fight it.

00:26:38.020 --> 00:26:39.390
Because it doesn't mean
you're a bad person.

00:26:39.390 --> 00:26:41.530
It's giving you this opportunity
to learn something new,

00:26:41.530 --> 00:26:42.510
which is actually cool.

00:26:42.510 --> 00:26:43.870
You can be this like nerd

00:26:43.870 --> 00:26:45.580
in the like social justice classroom

00:26:45.580 --> 00:26:48.230
where you're like, "Oh, this is the thing

00:26:48.230 --> 00:26:49.420
that wasn't on my radar yet,

00:26:49.420 --> 00:26:51.806
how can I learn to do better there?"

00:26:51.806 --> 00:26:52.970
- I think that's a great way to put it.

00:26:52.970 --> 00:26:54.048
I never thought of it that way.

00:26:54.048 --> 00:26:55.850
But I think that's sort
of what I've become.

00:26:55.850 --> 00:26:59.140
It's not that I don't like
sometimes lay awake at night,

00:26:59.140 --> 00:27:00.260
mortified by it.

00:27:00.260 --> 00:27:05.260
And it's by no means a
game, but there's this like,

00:27:06.240 --> 00:27:08.410
satisfaction with getting better at it.

00:27:08.410 --> 00:27:11.610
And knowing that the harm
I've done in the past

00:27:11.610 --> 00:27:14.687
I can lessen that in the future.

00:27:14.687 --> 00:27:17.133
- It's worth noting that this is hard.

00:27:17.133 --> 00:27:19.130
I mean, a lot of the
social psychology research

00:27:19.130 --> 00:27:20.900
suggests it's really easy to fall

00:27:20.900 --> 00:27:22.500
into these fixed mindset patterns

00:27:22.500 --> 00:27:24.830
where we kind of go off and deny stuff.

00:27:24.830 --> 00:27:27.220
And so just to get some of
the science on the table,

00:27:27.220 --> 00:27:29.530
talk to me about one of these ways

00:27:29.530 --> 00:27:32.560
that we end up denying privilege
in this interesting way

00:27:32.560 --> 00:27:33.480
and which has a funny name,

00:27:33.480 --> 00:27:35.810
it's called this bias
called "A Hard Knock Life."

00:27:35.810 --> 00:27:37.870
Let's just talk through that
to get it on the table here.

00:27:37.870 --> 00:27:39.420
- Oh, sure. I love that study.

00:27:39.420 --> 00:27:42.130
It's by my colleague, Taylor Phillips

00:27:42.130 --> 00:27:47.130
set at NYU and her co author,
Brian Lowry at Stanford.

00:27:47.870 --> 00:27:49.700
I love that paper.

00:27:49.700 --> 00:27:51.200
So, what they talk about

00:27:51.200 --> 00:27:52.210
with the heart nightlife effect...

00:27:52.210 --> 00:27:55.530
So, first, this context,
the phrase 'hard knock life'

00:27:55.530 --> 00:27:58.180
is referring to a song
in a Broadway musical

00:27:58.180 --> 00:28:00.172
in a movie called "Annie."

00:28:00.172 --> 00:28:04.405
- Don't worry. (laughs)

00:28:04.405 --> 00:28:05.488
- You sing it

00:28:06.651 --> 00:28:10.780
♪ It's a hard-knock life for us ♪

00:28:10.780 --> 00:28:13.533
- Folks, thank you for coming.

00:28:14.500 --> 00:28:15.650
That's awesome, Laurie.

00:28:16.830 --> 00:28:19.030
Anyway, that's the third
time I've got someone to sing

00:28:19.030 --> 00:28:22.930
live in the last week
on a Zoom of some sort.

00:28:22.930 --> 00:28:25.050
So I'm keeping track.

00:28:25.050 --> 00:28:26.451
So in the song that you just heard,

00:28:26.451 --> 00:28:28.060
"It's a hard knock life,"

00:28:28.060 --> 00:28:30.900
it refers to this sort of the challenges,

00:28:30.900 --> 00:28:33.980
adversity that one's face,
particularly in childhood.

00:28:33.980 --> 00:28:36.850
So in this particular set of studies,

00:28:36.850 --> 00:28:40.080
what they did was they asked people

00:28:40.080 --> 00:28:41.650
to think about their childhood

00:28:41.650 --> 00:28:43.830
and to reflect on the hardships

00:28:43.830 --> 00:28:46.090
and adversity of their childhoods.

00:28:46.090 --> 00:28:49.690
But the variable that they
manipulated in their studies

00:28:49.690 --> 00:28:52.670
is sometimes they just ask people to do it

00:28:52.670 --> 00:28:54.443
and sometimes, and I believe,

00:28:56.050 --> 00:28:57.440
can't remember if they
ran white participants

00:28:57.440 --> 00:28:58.273
and black participants,

00:28:58.273 --> 00:29:00.810
but they definitely have white
participants in the study.

00:29:00.810 --> 00:29:04.073
So, in one condition, they simply said,

00:29:05.412 --> 00:29:07.237
"Tell me about your childhood
and diversity and what..."

00:29:07.237 --> 00:29:09.630
And in the other condition,

00:29:09.630 --> 00:29:11.120
the instructions were slightly different

00:29:11.120 --> 00:29:12.720
for those participants.

00:29:12.720 --> 00:29:15.180
Before they said, "Tell
me about your childhood."

00:29:15.180 --> 00:29:19.150
They gave them a quick little sentence

00:29:19.150 --> 00:29:22.300
that said something about the advantages

00:29:22.300 --> 00:29:24.800
that white people have had

00:29:25.820 --> 00:29:27.950
versus blacks in the United States.

00:29:27.950 --> 00:29:29.420
And so it's basically a statement

00:29:29.420 --> 00:29:30.960
of what we would call group advantage,

00:29:30.960 --> 00:29:32.920
something you individually
had those advantages,

00:29:32.920 --> 00:29:34.320
but as a group.

00:29:34.320 --> 00:29:37.260
And what then? They looked
at what the responses were.

00:29:37.260 --> 00:29:38.093
And what they found

00:29:38.093 --> 00:29:42.390
was the people essentially
rewrote their personal histories.

00:29:42.390 --> 00:29:44.610
It doesn't even appear that it
was necessarily a conscious,

00:29:44.610 --> 00:29:45.443
like, they don't have evidence,

00:29:45.443 --> 00:29:46.360
there was a conscious process,

00:29:46.360 --> 00:29:49.660
but then they literally
cited more challenges

00:29:49.660 --> 00:29:51.500
and more adversity in their lives

00:29:51.500 --> 00:29:53.930
in the condition where they were reminded

00:29:53.930 --> 00:29:55.430
of group advantage.

00:29:55.430 --> 00:29:59.810
And so, what I take
away from these studies

00:29:59.810 --> 00:30:04.810
is this, how hard our minds will work,

00:30:05.720 --> 00:30:08.860
even without us directing it consciously

00:30:08.860 --> 00:30:12.690
to protect our identities,
that good person identity,

00:30:12.690 --> 00:30:15.200
that deserving person identity,

00:30:15.200 --> 00:30:20.200
that I have not taken any
advantage away from anyone else.

00:30:20.670 --> 00:30:25.610
And so, in those studies,
what people were revealing,

00:30:25.610 --> 00:30:27.140
I think if I remember,
right, in the papers,

00:30:27.140 --> 00:30:31.040
they were, the scholars were saying that

00:30:31.040 --> 00:30:32.120
while group advantage,

00:30:32.120 --> 00:30:34.040
a lot of people buy into group advantage,

00:30:34.040 --> 00:30:37.250
they found evidence that people
didn't necessarily dispute

00:30:37.250 --> 00:30:40.903
that there were these group
advantages based on race.

00:30:41.800 --> 00:30:46.120
What they resisted was
that they individually

00:30:46.120 --> 00:30:48.060
incurred that group advantage.

00:30:48.060 --> 00:30:51.030
And so that's where this hard knock life

00:30:51.030 --> 00:30:53.543
retelling of our personal
histories kicks in.

00:30:55.870 --> 00:30:58.180
There's some like, work
we have to do around.

00:30:58.180 --> 00:31:02.200
We intellectually understand
a lot of this data,

00:31:02.200 --> 00:31:04.730
but do we really internally accept

00:31:04.730 --> 00:31:07.640
that we may be beneficiaries of it,

00:31:07.640 --> 00:31:12.020
that we may be, in fact,
even propagating it.

00:31:12.020 --> 00:31:13.690
And the data suggests we are.

00:31:13.690 --> 00:31:17.139
We are beneficiaries and we
are propagating it, you know,

00:31:17.139 --> 00:31:20.280
depending on what dimension of
identity we're talking about,

00:31:20.280 --> 00:31:21.390
but if we're not black,

00:31:21.390 --> 00:31:24.680
we're probably benefiting
from some biases,

00:31:24.680 --> 00:31:28.223
racial biases that are
deeply hurting others.

00:31:29.120 --> 00:31:31.460
- And in the power that, you
know, without us realizing it,

00:31:31.460 --> 00:31:33.690
our minds are just gonna go to data,

00:31:33.690 --> 00:31:35.690
like, they're just
gonna look up statistics

00:31:35.690 --> 00:31:38.470
that make us feel like we personally

00:31:38.470 --> 00:31:40.690
couldn't have been as privileged,
like other white people,

00:31:40.690 --> 00:31:43.046
other streets and wall, you know,

00:31:43.046 --> 00:31:44.360
other, you know, Ivy
League, you know, employees,

00:31:44.360 --> 00:31:46.546
like they benefit, but not me, personally.

00:31:46.546 --> 00:31:48.713
- That right.
- Absolutely.

00:31:49.870 --> 00:31:51.940
This is all in a domain of, you know,

00:31:51.940 --> 00:31:53.320
trying to have a growth mindset

00:31:53.320 --> 00:31:55.810
so that when things
are pointed out to you,

00:31:55.810 --> 00:31:58.140
like, you can not deny them,

00:31:58.140 --> 00:31:59.850
you cannot try to like, you know,

00:31:59.850 --> 00:32:01.590
find other evidence and so on.

00:32:01.590 --> 00:32:02.480
But there are other ways

00:32:02.480 --> 00:32:05.840
that are kind of good person
mindset gets in the way too,

00:32:05.840 --> 00:32:08.680
even in cases where
we're like trying to act.

00:32:08.680 --> 00:32:10.600
And that's kind of what
you refer to in the book

00:32:10.600 --> 00:32:12.290
as something like cookie seeking.

00:32:12.290 --> 00:32:13.810
- Oh, yeah.
- Yeah.

00:32:13.810 --> 00:32:15.030
So talk a little bit about this,

00:32:15.030 --> 00:32:16.620
'cause these are cases where,

00:32:16.620 --> 00:32:18.183
we're not denying like,

00:32:19.299 --> 00:32:21.060
we're trying to take action to fix things.

00:32:21.060 --> 00:32:23.430
But we're doing this in a way that's like,

00:32:23.430 --> 00:32:25.390
a little bit kind of benefiting us,

00:32:25.390 --> 00:32:27.110
rather than the people
we're trying to help.

00:32:27.110 --> 00:32:28.580
- Yes, absolutely.

00:32:28.580 --> 00:32:30.610
Yeah, so the freeze cookies, again,

00:32:30.610 --> 00:32:32.400
is another one where I don't
know who to attribute it to,

00:32:32.400 --> 00:32:34.610
we tried hard to figure
out the original source,

00:32:34.610 --> 00:32:37.270
but it's a phrase that
is used pretty widely

00:32:37.270 --> 00:32:39.630
in activist communities.

00:32:39.630 --> 00:32:43.340
And it speaks to this
centering we sometimes do.

00:32:43.340 --> 00:32:48.340
So, you know, let's say,
I have a black colleague,

00:32:48.400 --> 00:32:53.400
and, you know, I've lately
been trying to educate myself,

00:32:54.108 --> 00:32:56.937
I went and read "The New Jim Crow."

00:32:57.960 --> 00:33:02.090
I read "Cast," I read "The
Warmth of Other Suns."

00:33:02.090 --> 00:33:04.890
So, I sort of somehow find a way

00:33:04.890 --> 00:33:07.160
to work it into my conversation.

00:33:07.160 --> 00:33:10.563
In this example, I'm not
black. I'm me, I'm not black.

00:33:11.420 --> 00:33:15.070
I somehow find a way to
work it into a conversation

00:33:15.070 --> 00:33:17.280
with my black colleague, you know,

00:33:17.280 --> 00:33:18.860
'cause I'm sort of waiting for them.

00:33:18.860 --> 00:33:21.430
You know, again, it may
not be totally conscious,

00:33:21.430 --> 00:33:24.580
but, I say it and then I like
do that little micro pause,

00:33:24.580 --> 00:33:28.520
where you're waiting to be
like, "Oh, oh, good for you."

00:33:28.520 --> 00:33:30.810
Like, that's a little bit of
a cookie seeking behavior.

00:33:30.810 --> 00:33:32.930
You know, I noticed myself even doing it,

00:33:32.930 --> 00:33:34.950
like outside of issues around identity

00:33:34.950 --> 00:33:36.900
even in like my own marriage, you know,

00:33:37.760 --> 00:33:39.550
my husband right now, during the pandemic,

00:33:39.550 --> 00:33:42.240
his work is outside the home.

00:33:42.240 --> 00:33:43.950
So really, I should be
feeling bad for him.

00:33:43.950 --> 00:33:45.870
He's, you know, an
essential worker out there.

00:33:45.870 --> 00:33:47.770
But, instead, I'm home with the kids.

00:33:47.770 --> 00:33:50.740
And so when he gets home, I
sort of do all sorts of like,

00:33:50.740 --> 00:33:53.150
I don't overtly whine.

00:33:53.150 --> 00:33:55.470
But I kind of drop in
a whole bunch of like,

00:33:55.470 --> 00:33:58.210
how hard today was kind of little weeding

00:33:58.210 --> 00:34:01.400
with those little micro
pauses to be validated,

00:34:01.400 --> 00:34:03.090
to be given a cookie.

00:34:03.090 --> 00:34:06.360
And that's all fine and good,

00:34:06.360 --> 00:34:09.180
except for the fact that
what I've just done there

00:34:09.180 --> 00:34:13.360
is instead of centering the
people I'm trying to support,

00:34:13.360 --> 00:34:15.580
if we go back to my black colleague,

00:34:15.580 --> 00:34:16.720
I'm reading those books,

00:34:16.720 --> 00:34:21.140
because I am trying to do
better when it comes to racism.

00:34:21.140 --> 00:34:21.973
But instead, what I've done

00:34:21.973 --> 00:34:24.530
is just added to the burden of racism,

00:34:24.530 --> 00:34:27.350
because I've now asked this colleague

00:34:27.350 --> 00:34:30.040
to see me and validate
me and it's exhausting.

00:34:30.040 --> 00:34:32.800
And it happens on a constant basis,

00:34:32.800 --> 00:34:34.180
in big ways and small ways.

00:34:34.180 --> 00:34:35.450
That's what cookie seeking is.

00:34:35.450 --> 00:34:37.540
And the story I tell in the book about it

00:34:37.540 --> 00:34:42.140
is a former student of mine, actually,

00:34:42.140 --> 00:34:46.443
who identifies as queer
and worked at the time

00:34:46.443 --> 00:34:48.850
in a very progressive
company in San Francisco

00:34:48.850 --> 00:34:50.100
in the Bay Area.

00:34:50.100 --> 00:34:52.820
And the Orlando police shooting happened.

00:34:52.820 --> 00:34:55.570
You might remember we woke
up that Sunday morning

00:34:55.570 --> 00:34:57.500
to that news.

00:34:57.500 --> 00:35:00.030
She was grief, stricken all day Sunday

00:35:00.030 --> 00:35:02.120
and then a Sunday night approached,

00:35:02.120 --> 00:35:04.360
and she thought about
going to work on Monday,

00:35:04.360 --> 00:35:07.260
and a company that had all
sorts of progressive policies

00:35:07.260 --> 00:35:10.400
and progressive people and like, you know,

00:35:10.400 --> 00:35:12.540
she was dreading going to work.

00:35:12.540 --> 00:35:13.373
Why was she dreading?

00:35:13.373 --> 00:35:15.040
Not because people wouldn't care,

00:35:15.040 --> 00:35:17.170
not because there would be bigotry,

00:35:17.170 --> 00:35:20.200
but because there would
be so much cookie seeking.

00:35:20.200 --> 00:35:22.350
She knew everybody will be coming to her,

00:35:22.350 --> 00:35:25.037
trying to be seen, trying to be validated.

00:35:25.037 --> 00:35:28.330
"Oh, you know, I am donating
to the Trevor Project

00:35:28.330 --> 00:35:32.280
for years, or, you know,
my gay college roommate."

00:35:32.280 --> 00:35:36.760
And she felt she had no
more emotional reserves

00:35:36.760 --> 00:35:40.290
to support them, that she
needed to be seen and heard,

00:35:40.290 --> 00:35:42.470
rather than helping them be seen or heard.

00:35:42.470 --> 00:35:44.470
So that's what cookie seeking refers to.

00:35:44.470 --> 00:35:45.700
- Yeah, and I think that this is something

00:35:45.700 --> 00:35:48.100
that comes up a lot, I
think, for our black students

00:35:48.100 --> 00:35:49.650
and staff members in the context of

00:35:49.650 --> 00:35:51.760
what we've been going through in 2020,

00:35:51.760 --> 00:35:53.990
where, again, well intentioned people

00:35:53.990 --> 00:35:55.570
who are believers in equity,

00:35:55.570 --> 00:35:56.787
but because they kind of have that,

00:35:56.787 --> 00:35:58.850
"I wanna be seen as a
good person mindset."

00:35:58.850 --> 00:36:01.930
End up putting more emotional
labor on black people,

00:36:01.930 --> 00:36:03.010
because they're trying to like,

00:36:03.010 --> 00:36:06.910
show off how woke they are
about these kinds of things.

00:36:06.910 --> 00:36:08.083
- Exactly, well said.

00:36:09.397 --> 00:36:10.930
- And so, all of these things

00:36:10.930 --> 00:36:14.160
from denying the privilege to
kind of seeking out some data

00:36:14.160 --> 00:36:15.690
that makes you feel better
about your privilege,

00:36:15.690 --> 00:36:17.940
Oh, it wasn't me to sort
of hard knock like effect.

00:36:17.940 --> 00:36:19.000
Even the cookie seeking,

00:36:19.000 --> 00:36:20.800
these are all cases where like,

00:36:20.800 --> 00:36:22.860
'cause we really, really
wanna be a good person,

00:36:22.860 --> 00:36:25.820
we like deeply get it wrong,
even though we're a believer.

00:36:25.820 --> 00:36:28.140
And so now I want to stretch
to strategies we can use

00:36:28.140 --> 00:36:29.740
to like be the builder, right?

00:36:29.740 --> 00:36:32.190
Like not just rely on the giving part,

00:36:32.190 --> 00:36:34.190
and kind of use that goodish mindset

00:36:34.190 --> 00:36:35.700
to kind of get a little bit further.

00:36:35.700 --> 00:36:38.100
And so I wanna start with
that on the privilege side

00:36:38.100 --> 00:36:40.370
about just the act of seeing it.

00:36:40.370 --> 00:36:41.550
So what are some tips,

00:36:41.550 --> 00:36:43.710
given that we know we're likely to deny it

00:36:43.710 --> 00:36:46.110
or just lots of things about
the headwinds and tailwinds

00:36:46.110 --> 00:36:47.720
mean, we don't see these things,

00:36:47.720 --> 00:36:50.510
what can you do to like, you
know, actually look there,

00:36:50.510 --> 00:36:52.600
to kind of take your spy
goggles on and put it on

00:36:52.600 --> 00:36:54.520
and see the privilege and the structures

00:36:54.520 --> 00:36:56.100
that you're benefiting from?

00:36:56.100 --> 00:36:57.540
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:36:57.540 --> 00:36:59.200
I think the first thing we can do

00:36:59.200 --> 00:37:02.380
is really just start noticing
what's happening around us.

00:37:02.380 --> 00:37:06.450
I mean, this is the classic,
you know, yesterday,

00:37:06.450 --> 00:37:09.130
I said, you know, "Gosh, for some reason,

00:37:09.130 --> 00:37:11.630
we have no pens in this house,
can I find a working pen?"

00:37:11.630 --> 00:37:12.463
And my husband says,

00:37:12.463 --> 00:37:14.610
"Oh, I just bought some pens,
they're in the junk drawers."

00:37:14.610 --> 00:37:16.957
I open the junk drawer and I'm like,

00:37:16.957 --> 00:37:19.130
"There is no pens in this junk drawer."

00:37:19.130 --> 00:37:20.370
Like I am looking, there's no any.

00:37:20.370 --> 00:37:21.377
He comes over and he's like,

00:37:21.377 --> 00:37:24.060
"Yeah, you mean this
entire pack of big pens

00:37:24.060 --> 00:37:25.630
right in front of you!"

00:37:25.630 --> 00:37:27.430
So part of it is we got to start seeing

00:37:27.430 --> 00:37:29.060
the big pens right in front of us.

00:37:29.060 --> 00:37:31.380
And the way I think we
can do that as some audits

00:37:31.380 --> 00:37:35.700
around what is like the daily
consumption we're doing?

00:37:35.700 --> 00:37:40.460
So whatever it is, you consume,
movies, podcasts, TV shows,

00:37:40.460 --> 00:37:43.900
music, you read, whatever...

00:37:43.900 --> 00:37:47.780
We're all consuming
something in some form, news.

00:37:47.780 --> 00:37:52.780
Audit the last 10 units
of that that you did.

00:37:52.960 --> 00:37:55.230
How much diversity of voice is in there?

00:37:55.230 --> 00:37:57.530
How much diversity of identity?

00:37:57.530 --> 00:38:01.190
How similar are those
identities to your own?

00:38:01.190 --> 00:38:02.440
Look at your social media...

00:38:02.440 --> 00:38:03.273
Sorry, I forgot to say,

00:38:03.273 --> 00:38:05.820
social media is a really
big piece of consumption

00:38:05.820 --> 00:38:06.993
for many of us.

00:38:08.030 --> 00:38:11.699
One really concrete place
we can start is to say,

00:38:11.699 --> 00:38:16.699
let's audit my last 10 units
of x and figure out if in fact,

00:38:17.190 --> 00:38:19.840
there isn't enough range of perspectives

00:38:19.840 --> 00:38:20.870
and identities in there

00:38:20.870 --> 00:38:22.550
and nothing different from ourselves.

00:38:22.550 --> 00:38:25.710
And for almost all of us, there isn't.

00:38:25.710 --> 00:38:28.110
I can actively start changing that.

00:38:28.110 --> 00:38:30.460
That's an action we can easily take.

00:38:30.460 --> 00:38:32.450
By doing that, we start to notice things

00:38:32.450 --> 00:38:33.540
we haven't noticed before.

00:38:33.540 --> 00:38:36.610
You know, I tell a story in the
book about when I was a kid,

00:38:36.610 --> 00:38:39.470
I had parents sort of
passed down this clock radio

00:38:39.470 --> 00:38:41.560
that was that was broken.

00:38:41.560 --> 00:38:43.560
And it turned out where it broke

00:38:43.560 --> 00:38:46.520
was on a particular black radio station.

00:38:46.520 --> 00:38:48.750
And so as a young kid, I
didn't really understand

00:38:48.750 --> 00:38:50.670
that there were like
different radio stations

00:38:50.670 --> 00:38:52.700
for white music and black music.

00:38:52.700 --> 00:38:57.140
So, I grew up hearing like
black talk radio in the morning

00:38:57.140 --> 00:38:59.900
and black music, of course,

00:38:59.900 --> 00:39:03.220
but also black tellings of news events.

00:39:03.220 --> 00:39:05.970
And, of course, it was a wide
range of black perspectives

00:39:05.970 --> 00:39:07.250
that I heard on that station.

00:39:07.250 --> 00:39:10.650
And when I got old enough to realize

00:39:10.650 --> 00:39:13.040
that there was more than one
radio station in the world

00:39:13.040 --> 00:39:14.450
and that other radio stations

00:39:14.450 --> 00:39:16.280
where sort of saying different things,

00:39:16.280 --> 00:39:20.130
it was this like mo noticing moment.

00:39:20.130 --> 00:39:23.860
So I'd say, right there is
one place we can start today

00:39:23.860 --> 00:39:26.350
to begin to notice things
we haven't seen before

00:39:26.350 --> 00:39:29.050
the perspectives we
haven't tuned into before.

00:39:29.050 --> 00:39:30.240
And last thing I'll say on that,

00:39:30.240 --> 00:39:32.850
particularly if it's on social
media that you're doing this,

00:39:32.850 --> 00:39:35.100
when we start to tune in to conversations

00:39:35.100 --> 00:39:37.050
we weren't hearing before,

00:39:37.050 --> 00:39:39.550
it's very important that we treat it

00:39:39.550 --> 00:39:42.580
as if you were eavesdropping
in a coffee shop,

00:39:42.580 --> 00:39:44.560
chances are by politeness norms,

00:39:44.560 --> 00:39:47.840
you wouldn't insert your
self into that conversation.

00:39:47.840 --> 00:39:49.973
You are a guest, you're an eavesdropper.

00:39:50.918 --> 00:39:54.360
So, the opportunity is
to listen not to argue,

00:39:54.360 --> 00:39:58.250
not to justify, not to say
all white people, all women,

00:39:58.250 --> 00:39:59.820
all men, whatever.

00:39:59.820 --> 00:40:02.340
It's Simply to pick up
stuff we're not hearing.

00:40:02.340 --> 00:40:04.790
- And I think another thing you
said in that piece of advice

00:40:04.790 --> 00:40:06.710
I wanted to kind of like double down on,

00:40:06.710 --> 00:40:09.933
which is that you said, kind
of consuming these things,

00:40:10.820 --> 00:40:13.700
you didn't say, you know, talk
to your one queer colleague

00:40:13.700 --> 00:40:14.900
about what it's like to be queer

00:40:14.900 --> 00:40:16.610
or demand that your black colleague, like,

00:40:16.610 --> 00:40:18.030
explain to you how she's feeling

00:40:18.030 --> 00:40:20.070
after the most recent police shooting.

00:40:20.070 --> 00:40:23.380
Like there's content that
already exists out there,

00:40:23.380 --> 00:40:25.640
you shouldn't ask people
for emotional labor

00:40:25.640 --> 00:40:27.779
to tell you what to look at.

00:40:27.779 --> 00:40:28.860
- That's right.

00:40:28.860 --> 00:40:30.860
There's no shortage of...

00:40:32.780 --> 00:40:34.950
Which isn't to say that
we should be silent,

00:40:34.950 --> 00:40:39.950
what we wanna do is, if
there's a terrible shooting

00:40:40.180 --> 00:40:42.280
of an unarmed black person,

00:40:42.280 --> 00:40:45.777
there is value in saying
to a black colleague,

00:40:45.777 --> 00:40:46.710
"I just want you to know,

00:40:46.710 --> 00:40:48.490
I'm thinking of you and your family.

00:40:48.490 --> 00:40:49.820
Is there anything I can do to support you?

00:40:49.820 --> 00:40:52.000
Is there anything I can
take off your plate today?

00:40:52.000 --> 00:40:53.650
Like, I just want you to know,

00:40:53.650 --> 00:40:55.080
I don't know how you're doing today.

00:40:55.080 --> 00:40:56.850
But I just want you to know, I see you."

00:40:56.850 --> 00:40:58.210
There may be value in that,

00:40:58.210 --> 00:40:59.090
of course, you have to judge

00:40:59.090 --> 00:41:00.810
the individual relationship and context.

00:41:00.810 --> 00:41:03.350
So, silence is actually quite
destructive those moments,

00:41:03.350 --> 00:41:04.873
but that's different than saying,

00:41:04.873 --> 00:41:08.520
"Can you tell me how you feel
when you get pulled over?"

00:41:08.520 --> 00:41:10.333
I mean, like, that's asking a lot.

00:41:10.333 --> 00:41:11.710
Then why don't understand.

00:41:11.710 --> 00:41:15.000
But if you just, you know,
put your hands, you know,

00:41:15.000 --> 00:41:16.570
like, that's emotional labor.

00:41:16.570 --> 00:41:19.010
- Right, right. And so
that's one distinction.

00:41:19.010 --> 00:41:21.610
But I think a bigger thing
I think we need to do

00:41:21.610 --> 00:41:25.630
to kind of be builders is like
how we deal with the moments

00:41:25.630 --> 00:41:27.400
when our mistakes are pointed out.

00:41:27.400 --> 00:41:28.580
And one of the things,

00:41:28.580 --> 00:41:30.940
I think you're incredibly
frank about in the book,

00:41:30.940 --> 00:41:33.250
is that one of the reasons we
can't be good people right now

00:41:33.250 --> 00:41:35.820
is that we're probably all gonna mess up.

00:41:35.820 --> 00:41:39.820
You're not yourself, if you
are yourself from any identity

00:41:39.820 --> 00:41:41.470
that is not marginalized.

00:41:41.470 --> 00:41:42.910
Because of the headwind tailwind thing,

00:41:42.910 --> 00:41:46.194
you're not gonna see stuff,
and you are gonna screw up.

00:41:46.194 --> 00:41:47.861
Is that?
- 100%.

00:41:49.990 --> 00:41:51.900
I had a conversation with somebody

00:41:51.900 --> 00:41:55.870
I really respect last week,
who is reading my book,

00:41:55.870 --> 00:41:57.790
or it just was in the
middle of reading my book

00:41:57.790 --> 00:41:59.540
when I spoke to her.

00:41:59.540 --> 00:42:01.940
This is someone I don't know well.

00:42:01.940 --> 00:42:02.910
I know of her.

00:42:02.910 --> 00:42:05.060
And this was the first time
I was speaking with her.

00:42:05.060 --> 00:42:10.060
And she said, "Can I share
with you? I love your book.

00:42:10.110 --> 00:42:13.660
But I noticed you centre whiteness?"

00:42:13.660 --> 00:42:17.690
And I was like, "Oh! All right.
What do you mean by that?"

00:42:17.690 --> 00:42:21.950
And she proceeded to read a
section of my book aloud to me,

00:42:21.950 --> 00:42:23.280
in which it was really clear,

00:42:23.280 --> 00:42:25.170
I had centered whiteness
in the way I had done it

00:42:25.170 --> 00:42:28.260
is I had in that particular
section of the book.

00:42:28.260 --> 00:42:30.640
I hadn't even like named
that I was doing it.

00:42:30.640 --> 00:42:32.970
But I was speaking to white people,

00:42:32.970 --> 00:42:34.510
without even naming I was doing it.

00:42:34.510 --> 00:42:38.190
It was just sort of as if
the default is whiteness,

00:42:38.190 --> 00:42:40.677
and so I can now speak to
you without even saying,

00:42:40.677 --> 00:42:42.280
"Okay, white readers,

00:42:42.280 --> 00:42:45.247
this little bit might be more
useful to you than to others."

00:42:46.140 --> 00:42:47.880
I did not do that consciously.

00:42:47.880 --> 00:42:50.693
That is not the type of
book I meant to write.

00:42:51.786 --> 00:42:56.090
And yet, I'm not at all
surprised it happens, you know,

00:42:56.090 --> 00:42:58.780
I have also grown up trying to, you know,

00:42:58.780 --> 00:43:01.710
fit in into a community of whiteness.

00:43:01.710 --> 00:43:05.690
So, the response in that
moment is really critical

00:43:05.690 --> 00:43:10.320
of I felt the fixed mindset rising,

00:43:10.320 --> 00:43:12.857
but because I know to
look for it, I was like,

00:43:12.857 --> 00:43:14.411
"Oh, my gosh! What a gift."

00:43:14.411 --> 00:43:15.677
That's the moment I have saved myself,

00:43:15.677 --> 00:43:17.260
"What a gift. What a gift."

00:43:17.260 --> 00:43:19.790
She's telling me, "What a gift."

00:43:19.790 --> 00:43:22.150
She's probably the million
person who thought it

00:43:22.150 --> 00:43:24.350
but she's the one who's
actually telling me.

00:43:25.660 --> 00:43:27.930
- Walk me through kind of
what are the right reactions

00:43:27.930 --> 00:43:28.763
in those cases?

00:43:28.763 --> 00:43:31.610
Because I'm sure everybody
again, everybody on this webinar

00:43:31.610 --> 00:43:33.420
is gonna experience it for some identity

00:43:33.420 --> 00:43:35.050
that they might not see themselves.

00:43:35.050 --> 00:43:36.782
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:43:36.782 --> 00:43:39.280
If the situation is something
like the one I just gave,

00:43:39.280 --> 00:43:43.150
or somebody like live, or you
know, an email or whatever,

00:43:43.150 --> 00:43:44.540
giving you information

00:43:44.540 --> 00:43:47.273
about something they
noticed that you didn't,

00:43:48.410 --> 00:43:50.070
that's the key moment to say,

00:43:50.070 --> 00:43:52.310
first and foremost, it buys you time,

00:43:52.310 --> 00:43:53.650
and it's what you mean.

00:43:53.650 --> 00:43:54.483
Thank you.

00:43:55.470 --> 00:43:57.510
Thank you, first and foremost.

00:43:57.510 --> 00:43:58.733
Wow, thank you.

00:44:00.520 --> 00:44:02.423
The second thing to say is,

00:44:03.327 --> 00:44:05.140
"I wanna understand what you're saying."

00:44:05.140 --> 00:44:07.842
If you don't understand what
they're saying, you know,

00:44:07.842 --> 00:44:09.177
can you tell me like,

00:44:09.177 --> 00:44:11.987
"Oh, you center whiteness in the book."

00:44:11.987 --> 00:44:16.960
"Wow, thank you. Can you tell
me where you saw me do that?"

00:44:16.960 --> 00:44:19.250
The person may have
the stamina to do that.

00:44:19.250 --> 00:44:20.350
And they may not.

00:44:20.350 --> 00:44:24.250
And if they don't, that's
not on them. It's on me.

00:44:24.250 --> 00:44:27.297
But if you can get an example
not because you're like,

00:44:27.297 --> 00:44:28.890
"Oh, well prove it."

00:44:28.890 --> 00:44:31.470
It's more like, "Oh,
wow, I am really grateful

00:44:31.470 --> 00:44:32.470
you saw something, I did it."

00:44:32.470 --> 00:44:36.040
So language like, "Thank
you. Can you tell me more?

00:44:36.040 --> 00:44:37.360
I'm sorry."

00:44:37.360 --> 00:44:40.207
Let's not underestimate the
power of those three words.

00:44:40.207 --> 00:44:41.320
"I am sorry."

00:44:41.320 --> 00:44:42.970
I wanna be clear. It's three words.

00:44:42.970 --> 00:44:47.270
It's not, "I am sorry,
that that I offended you."

00:44:47.270 --> 00:44:50.020
Or, "I am sorry. You were offended."

00:44:50.020 --> 00:44:51.820
Or, "I am sorry."

00:44:51.820 --> 00:44:54.417
No, it's "I am sorry."

00:44:56.210 --> 00:44:57.730
We have to own our impact,

00:44:57.730 --> 00:44:59.500
even when it doesn't match our intention.

00:44:59.500 --> 00:45:00.610
In fact, it's especially

00:45:00.610 --> 00:45:02.740
when it doesn't match our intention.

00:45:02.740 --> 00:45:07.740
So I am sorry, I want to learn
more about that, you know,

00:45:08.360 --> 00:45:10.820
meaning I'm going to go
learn more about that,

00:45:10.820 --> 00:45:13.100
or that's really useful for me.

00:45:13.100 --> 00:45:15.240
I'm gonna educate myself on that,

00:45:15.240 --> 00:45:20.240
or I'm gonna go, review things
I've written in the past

00:45:21.020 --> 00:45:24.070
and see if I made that
same mistake, or, you know,

00:45:24.070 --> 00:45:27.190
when I have some data I could
look at to see, you know,

00:45:27.190 --> 00:45:30.540
you're saying that my
grading doesn't feel good,

00:45:30.540 --> 00:45:32.760
I'm gonna actually go back
and look at past grading

00:45:32.760 --> 00:45:35.430
and see if in fact, that
pattern does show up.

00:45:35.430 --> 00:45:38.410
And thank you for giving
me something to test.

00:45:38.410 --> 00:45:40.040
In the data I have, we all have data,

00:45:40.040 --> 00:45:41.540
no matter what you do for a living,

00:45:41.540 --> 00:45:44.540
you have some, whatever
you do is, in fact data,

00:45:44.540 --> 00:45:47.120
your daily actions and
daily behaviors are data,

00:45:47.120 --> 00:45:50.137
you may or may not be collecting
it and documenting it.

00:45:50.137 --> 00:45:53.630
But that is, in fact, data
that you could look at.

00:45:53.630 --> 00:45:56.660
So, let me look at it. Let
me learn more about it.

00:45:56.660 --> 00:45:58.810
Thank you, I am sorry.

00:45:58.810 --> 00:46:01.977
These are all great places
to start that conversation.

00:46:01.977 --> 00:46:04.180
- And so that's kind of when you mess up

00:46:04.180 --> 00:46:06.590
which is so important to
kind of react correctly,

00:46:06.590 --> 00:46:08.080
because we're going to mess up.

00:46:08.080 --> 00:46:10.130
But there are other things
we need to do as a builder,

00:46:10.130 --> 00:46:11.550
which is to step up, right?

00:46:11.550 --> 00:46:13.870
Which is to maybe be the one pointing out

00:46:13.870 --> 00:46:16.010
when we notice other cases of bias

00:46:16.010 --> 00:46:18.520
or when we notice other cases of mistreat.

00:46:18.520 --> 00:46:22.020
And you talk when somebody
who's not themselves

00:46:22.020 --> 00:46:24.570
from a marginalized
group, points this out,

00:46:24.570 --> 00:46:26.540
you talk about that in terms of

00:46:26.540 --> 00:46:28.130
what's called ordinary privilege.

00:46:28.130 --> 00:46:29.990
And so walk us through ordinary privilege

00:46:29.990 --> 00:46:31.340
and kind of why it's so important

00:46:31.340 --> 00:46:33.430
that people from non
marginalized identities

00:46:33.430 --> 00:46:35.260
use that to fix things.

00:46:35.260 --> 00:46:36.560
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:46:36.560 --> 00:46:37.910
So this brings us back a little bit

00:46:37.910 --> 00:46:39.170
to our headwinds and tailwind.

00:46:39.170 --> 00:46:42.110
