00:58:30.010 --> 00:58:31.330
we're usually talking,

00:58:31.330 --> 00:58:34.143
there's almost always words
coming out of our mouth.

00:58:36.190 --> 00:58:38.530
- Yeah, and then I think
that sort of builds up

00:58:38.530 --> 00:58:41.860
to like, another question,
which is this idea that,

00:58:41.860 --> 00:58:45.450
you know, if you're from a majority group,

00:58:45.450 --> 00:58:47.670
you're often benefiting from
these structures, right?

00:58:47.670 --> 00:58:49.400
Like when we think about
the structural changes

00:58:49.400 --> 00:58:52.310
that will make a Yale evening things out

00:58:52.310 --> 00:58:54.100
means that there's gonna be some people

00:58:54.100 --> 00:58:55.810
who are kind of having the tailwinds

00:58:55.810 --> 00:58:57.150
kind of go down a little bit.

00:58:57.150 --> 00:58:59.200
But there's this worry that
that might mean, you know,

00:58:59.200 --> 00:59:01.530
the headwinds are gonna
go down a little bit, too.

00:59:01.530 --> 00:59:03.070
And so what advice do you have

00:59:03.070 --> 00:59:05.970
for people who kind of want to
make these systemic changes,

00:59:05.970 --> 00:59:08.220
when they realize they might be kind of

00:59:08.220 --> 00:59:11.610
facing the possibility that
some people perceive, you know,

00:59:11.610 --> 00:59:13.310
things getting more just

00:59:13.310 --> 00:59:16.340
as things getting unfair,
you know, for the, like,

00:59:16.340 --> 00:59:18.830
the non marginalized folks
who kind of feel like,

00:59:18.830 --> 00:59:20.280
well, you know, why would I,

00:59:20.280 --> 00:59:21.650
as a white man have to talk less?

00:59:21.650 --> 00:59:23.580
Or why would I, as a straight
person have to, you know,

00:59:23.580 --> 00:59:24.413
think differently?

00:59:24.413 --> 00:59:26.930
Or why would I as able bodied
person have to worry about,

00:59:26.930 --> 00:59:29.220
you know, text on my screen
or something like that,

00:59:29.220 --> 00:59:30.250
like, what advice do you get

00:59:30.250 --> 00:59:32.580
when you kind of get that
criticism brought to you?

00:59:32.580 --> 00:59:36.960
- Yeah, well, I have
some unpublished data.

00:59:36.960 --> 00:59:39.100
So it's a paper we're still working on.

00:59:39.100 --> 00:59:41.450
But we've got some data that suggests that

00:59:41.450 --> 00:59:44.470
would help us stay engaged
or get more engaged

00:59:44.470 --> 00:59:49.360
in those moments, is connecting
it to a bigger picture.

00:59:51.854 --> 00:59:54.280
And this is very
consistent also with like,

00:59:54.280 --> 00:59:58.230
sort of classic work in psychology
around affirming the self

00:59:58.230 --> 01:00:00.490
and creating affirmations.

01:00:00.490 --> 01:00:01.540
I don't mean like affirmations,

01:00:01.540 --> 01:00:03.110
like things you stand in
the mirror and look at,

01:00:03.110 --> 01:00:04.187
and shave yourself,

01:00:04.187 --> 01:00:08.470
but just in the jargon of our field,

01:00:08.470 --> 01:00:11.100
sort of reflecting on your own values

01:00:11.100 --> 01:00:12.980
is one form of affirmation.

01:00:12.980 --> 01:00:14.700
And what research shows

01:00:14.700 --> 01:00:17.780
is that when you connect those moments

01:00:17.780 --> 01:00:19.460
that you're experiencing those

01:00:19.460 --> 01:00:21.810
that are specifically around you,

01:00:21.810 --> 01:00:24.170
and your interests and your ego,

01:00:24.170 --> 01:00:27.490
if you can connect it to a bigger picture,

01:00:27.490 --> 01:00:30.100
that seems to create
greater resilience for us,

01:00:30.100 --> 01:00:32.713
around our self image, around our ego.

01:00:36.134 --> 01:00:38.530
It is true in any one given moment,

01:00:38.530 --> 01:00:40.240
there might be a fixed pie, right?

01:00:40.240 --> 01:00:42.480
In any one given moment,
there might be, you know,

01:00:42.480 --> 01:00:45.863
one job or one, whatever.

01:00:46.810 --> 01:00:49.340
But in the grand scheme of things,

01:00:49.340 --> 01:00:53.790
we tend to underestimate
how much the pie can grow.

01:00:53.790 --> 01:00:56.660
In negotiations research,
they see this all the time,

01:00:56.660 --> 01:00:58.670
they call it the mythical fixed pie,

01:00:58.670 --> 01:01:02.263
that we think my loss is
your gain and vice versa.

01:01:03.140 --> 01:01:05.930
When in fact, there's actually
many more opportunities

01:01:05.930 --> 01:01:09.620
for multiple people,
myself and others to gain

01:01:09.620 --> 01:01:10.693
than I realize.

01:01:13.819 --> 01:01:17.073
If I'm to be a little more
kind of, pushy on this,

01:01:18.070 --> 01:01:22.350
I would say, I don't even
think we have the option

01:01:22.350 --> 01:01:23.790
to wait anymore.

01:01:23.790 --> 01:01:26.850
And I think that moment came and went,

01:01:26.850 --> 01:01:29.320
where you can sort of choose to opt out

01:01:29.320 --> 01:01:31.650
of these conversations.

01:01:31.650 --> 01:01:36.440
I think we're now in the
midst of dramatic change,

01:01:36.440 --> 01:01:41.097
long overdue, and it's time as a builder

01:01:43.920 --> 01:01:46.520
to sort of figure out
what your role is gonna be

01:01:46.520 --> 01:01:48.330
in advancing that change,

01:01:48.330 --> 01:01:51.340
as opposed to worrying about
if the change should happen,

01:01:51.340 --> 01:01:54.360
or if the change will benefit me.

01:01:54.360 --> 01:01:58.440
The change is a lot better
chance of benefiting you,

01:01:58.440 --> 01:02:02.053
if you're part of it, than
if you're resisting it.

01:02:03.060 --> 01:02:05.560
And so I think that's true,
no matter how you look at it

01:02:05.560 --> 01:02:08.020
in terms of cultural norms,

01:02:08.020 --> 01:02:10.690
in terms of demographics in this country,

01:02:10.690 --> 01:02:13.480
I think in terms of sort of what skills

01:02:13.480 --> 01:02:17.870
are being valued by employers,
and admissions officers,

01:02:17.870 --> 01:02:19.770
no matter how you look at it,

01:02:19.770 --> 01:02:22.150
one is better off taking the stance of

01:02:22.150 --> 01:02:25.360
I'm going to be building
this change as part of it,

01:02:25.360 --> 01:02:28.537
that as opposed to questioning
it or resisting it.

01:02:28.537 --> 01:02:30.450
- And then my final
question for you tonight,

01:02:30.450 --> 01:02:32.800
kind of talks a little
bit about where we are

01:02:32.800 --> 01:02:35.840
in 2020, right now, where,
you know, these conversations

01:02:35.840 --> 01:02:38.980
are more needed than ever,
more important than ever,

01:02:38.980 --> 01:02:40.850
but they can also be a
little bit exhausting.

01:02:40.850 --> 01:02:41.790
And so this is kind of ending

01:02:41.790 --> 01:02:43.220
with a little bit of a personal question.

01:02:43.220 --> 01:02:46.170
You are, you know, central
in this fight for justice,

01:02:46.170 --> 01:02:48.900
central in this fight, referring
to kind of getting people

01:02:48.900 --> 01:02:50.980
to think about being
goodish people, and so on.

01:02:50.980 --> 01:02:53.420
But that fight can be kind
of exhausting sometimes.

01:02:53.420 --> 01:02:55.890
And I think, you know, on
behalf of all of our activists

01:02:55.890 --> 01:02:57.890
is a number another set of questions

01:02:57.890 --> 01:02:59.220
that we got a lot of, it's like,

01:02:59.220 --> 01:03:00.840
how do you kind of
protect your mental health

01:03:00.840 --> 01:03:02.100
in the face of this stuff?

01:03:02.100 --> 01:03:03.500
You know, how do you
protect your mental health

01:03:03.500 --> 01:03:05.530
in the face of like, you
know, turning on the heat,

01:03:05.530 --> 01:03:07.710
when people really seem
like they're not listening?

01:03:07.710 --> 01:03:09.210
Or you think your giving light

01:03:09.210 --> 01:03:10.510
and your not doing the light version

01:03:10.510 --> 01:03:11.850
and getting a lot of pushback.

01:03:11.850 --> 01:03:13.040
Like, how do you protect yourself

01:03:13.040 --> 01:03:14.400
in the face of all this stuff?

01:03:14.400 --> 01:03:16.260
- Yeah, it really is exhausting.

01:03:16.260 --> 01:03:19.000
And it is important,
because we want people in it

01:03:19.000 --> 01:03:20.033
for the long haul.

01:03:21.310 --> 01:03:25.010
I wrote back in June, you know,

01:03:25.010 --> 01:03:28.470
maybe three weeks after
George Floyd was murdered,

01:03:28.470 --> 01:03:31.720
I decided to start this newsletter

01:03:31.720 --> 01:03:33.976
that you refer to
earlier, Dear Good People.

01:03:33.976 --> 01:03:37.070
And it was exactly for that reason,

01:03:37.070 --> 01:03:41.200
it was that I was seeing folks
who were already exhausted,

01:03:41.200 --> 01:03:43.440
now really exhausted,

01:03:43.440 --> 01:03:46.790
and then other people sort
of joining the conversation

01:03:46.790 --> 01:03:48.580
or trying to deepen their investment.

01:03:48.580 --> 01:03:50.730
And I was worried about

01:03:50.730 --> 01:03:53.260
by July, August, September, October,

01:03:53.260 --> 01:03:54.842
where was everyone gonna be?

01:03:54.842 --> 01:03:57.530
We're exhausted people
gonna be burned out?

01:03:57.530 --> 01:03:59.380
And where newer people

01:03:59.380 --> 01:04:01.980
going to have lost interest or confidence?

01:04:01.980 --> 01:04:05.580
So, I wrote this piece
called the 10% More Rule,

01:04:05.580 --> 01:04:09.090
and it's on my website,
dollychugh.com/newsletter,

01:04:09.090 --> 01:04:11.810
but I'll just summarize it.

01:04:11.810 --> 01:04:16.590
Basically, I think what we
all wanna do is 10% more,

01:04:16.590 --> 01:04:19.680
if you're new to this conversation,

01:04:19.680 --> 01:04:24.160
or new to this understanding,
then 10% more means

01:04:25.630 --> 01:04:27.860
be 10% more mortified.

01:04:27.860 --> 01:04:32.250
Like, don't look away, stay engaged.

01:04:32.250 --> 01:04:34.570
That means we need you to
keep reading and watching

01:04:34.570 --> 01:04:35.920
and listening and learning.

01:04:37.190 --> 01:04:40.270
If you've been in this
conversation, you know,

01:04:40.270 --> 01:04:43.270
you've hashtagged, you've
donated, you volunteered,

01:04:43.270 --> 01:04:44.850
that kind of thing,

01:04:44.850 --> 01:04:46.760
but, you know, if this
was a basketball game,

01:04:46.760 --> 01:04:49.500
you were spending more time
on the bench then in the game,

01:04:49.500 --> 01:04:51.210
taking hard fouls,

01:04:51.210 --> 01:04:54.530
now it's time to be 10% more terrified.

01:04:54.530 --> 01:04:57.500
That means, get in the game,
take a hard fought foul,

01:04:57.500 --> 01:05:01.240
take a risk, essentially,
start Saying things at work,

01:05:01.240 --> 01:05:04.220
start asking questions,
start reaching out,

01:05:04.220 --> 01:05:06.630
start spending your comfort.

01:05:06.630 --> 01:05:07.740
And then finally to the group,

01:05:07.740 --> 01:05:11.450
you just refer to those
who've been on the frontlines

01:05:11.450 --> 01:05:15.410
fighting so hard, a great
detriment to themselves at times,

01:05:15.410 --> 01:05:19.890
they're in every way physical,
emotional, financial,

01:05:19.890 --> 01:05:22.200
the 10% more refers to 10% more,

01:05:22.200 --> 01:05:24.870
and these, I'm gonna do
air quotes, "satisfied."

01:05:24.870 --> 01:05:26.163
And by that, I mean,

01:05:27.240 --> 01:05:30.440
not satisfied with the
state of affairs at all.

01:05:30.440 --> 01:05:33.203
But satisfied that you
can come out of the game,

01:05:35.313 --> 01:05:37.320
you don't have to play every minute,

01:05:37.320 --> 01:05:40.860
you can let everyone else
get in the game a little bit,

01:05:40.860 --> 01:05:43.370
learning about the game
and playing the game,

01:05:43.370 --> 01:05:45.860
you can step out and catch your breath

01:05:45.860 --> 01:05:50.100
10% more than you have
been, and then come back in.

01:05:50.100 --> 01:05:52.730
And I think that the key is that

01:05:52.730 --> 01:05:55.750
if we're going to be sustainable in this,

01:05:55.750 --> 01:05:58.203
one of the metaphors I share in the book

01:05:58.203 --> 01:06:01.690
that someone I interviewed
offered me is a flock of birds.

01:06:01.690 --> 01:06:04.260
Apparently, when you see
v formations of birds

01:06:04.260 --> 01:06:06.430
flying about this time of year,

01:06:06.430 --> 01:06:10.050
that the lead bird, the one
that's fighting those headwinds,

01:06:10.050 --> 01:06:12.750
is rotating, it isn't
always the same bird,

01:06:12.750 --> 01:06:14.170
which I did not know.

01:06:14.170 --> 01:06:15.630
But it's for this exact reason

01:06:15.630 --> 01:06:18.150
that if the lead bird was
gonna stay the lead bird

01:06:18.150 --> 01:06:20.500
all the way to their final destination,

01:06:20.500 --> 01:06:21.880
they literally aren't going to make it

01:06:21.880 --> 01:06:23.270
to the final destination.

01:06:23.270 --> 01:06:24.520
So let's rotate out.

01:06:24.520 --> 01:06:28.630
So, I would say 10% more,

01:06:28.630 --> 01:06:33.630
if you're on the front lines
means 10% less, essentially.

01:06:33.830 --> 01:06:37.000
Not forever, just catching your breath.

01:06:37.000 --> 01:06:39.290
- I think this is a
fantastic place to end,

01:06:39.290 --> 01:06:40.123
because honestly,

01:06:40.123 --> 01:06:42.460
I think if we really wanna
achieve belonging at Yale,

01:06:42.460 --> 01:06:46.120
if literally all the 100s and
100s of people on this call

01:06:46.120 --> 01:06:50.310
did 10% more at Yale, of
working to be a goodish person

01:06:50.310 --> 01:06:52.370
and fighting so that everyone at Yale

01:06:52.370 --> 01:06:53.640
feels like they belong,

01:06:53.640 --> 01:06:55.040
I feel like we'd make a lot of progress

01:06:55.040 --> 01:06:57.450
towards making this
institution a better place.

01:06:57.450 --> 01:07:00.438
But I wanted to end by
thanking Dolly so deeply,

01:07:00.438 --> 01:07:03.590
I hope you can all see now,
why she's so fantastic.

01:07:03.590 --> 01:07:05.250
And her work is so fantastic.

01:07:05.250 --> 01:07:06.810
Again, just to give her a couple plugs,

01:07:06.810 --> 01:07:08.290
you should check out her book,

01:07:08.290 --> 01:07:09.640
which I think she'll hold up right now

01:07:09.640 --> 01:07:10.967
if she's sitting right near it.

01:07:10.967 --> 01:07:12.770
Her book, "The Person You Mean to Be:

01:07:12.770 --> 01:07:14.300
How Good People Fight Bias."

01:07:14.300 --> 01:07:16.850
It's fantastic and really worth the read.

01:07:16.850 --> 01:07:19.240
You can also even if you
need more bite sized chunks,

01:07:19.240 --> 01:07:21.120
check out her newsletter
which she mentioned,

01:07:21.120 --> 01:07:23.447
Dear Good People, which you can check out

01:07:23.447 --> 01:07:25.960
@dollychugh.com/newsletter.

01:07:25.960 --> 01:07:27.920
Thank you everyone for joining today

01:07:27.920 --> 01:07:29.254
and for joining the fight

01:07:29.254 --> 01:07:33.050
to get Yale to be a place
where everyone belongs.

01:07:33.050 --> 01:07:36.950
I hope I will see you at the
next anti-racism webinar,

01:07:36.950 --> 01:07:39.677
stay tuned to Yale news where
you'll hear more about that.

01:07:39.677 --> 01:07:41.540
Ibram X. Kendi will also be fantastic.

01:07:41.540 --> 01:07:43.100
And thank you to all the organizers

01:07:43.100 --> 01:07:44.160
for putting this together.

01:07:44.160 --> 01:07:46.560
Really appreciate having
you here today. Thanks.

